{
  "schema": "lessonlens/extract@1",
  "source": "eval/slides/en-04-shortest-paths.pptx",
  "kind": "pptx",
  "title": "Shortest Paths in Graphs",
  "lang": "en",
  "pages": [
    {
      "index": 1,
      "title": "Problem Statement",
      "size": [
        13.333,
        7.5
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Problem Statement",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.45,
            12.1,
            1.05
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Given a weighted graph, find the minimum-cost path from a source s to every vertex.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "The cost of a path is the sum of its edge weights.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "A distance is infinite when the vertex is unreachable.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "title": "Breadth-First Search",
      "size": [
        13.333,
        7.5
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Breadth-First Search",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.45,
            12.1,
            1.05
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "BFS explores vertices in order of hop count.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "On an unweighted graph it computes shortest paths in O(V + E).",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "It needs a FIFO queue and a visited set.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "title": "Dijkstra: Idea",
      "size": [
        13.333,
        7.5
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Dijkstra: Idea",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.45,
            12.1,
            1.05
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Keep a tentative distance for every vertex: 0 for the source, infinity elsewhere.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Repeatedly extract the unvisited vertex with the smallest tentative distance and relax its edges.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "It is correct only when all edge weights are non-negative.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "title": "Dijkstra: Complexity",
      "size": [
        13.333,
        7.5
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Dijkstra: Complexity",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.45,
            12.1,
            1.05
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "With a binary heap the running time is O((V + E) log V).",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "With a Fibonacci heap it becomes O(E + V log V).",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "The relaxation code is identical regardless of the priority queue.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "title": "Negative Edges",
      "size": [
        13.333,
        7.5
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Negative Edges",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.45,
            12.1,
            1.05
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Dijkstra may finalise a vertex too early when a negative edge exists.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Bellman-Ford relaxes every edge V - 1 times and runs in O(V E).",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Bellman-Ford also detects negative cycles: one more successful relaxation proves the answer is undefined.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "title": "All-Pairs Shortest Paths",
      "size": [
        13.333,
        7.5
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "All-Pairs Shortest Paths",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.45,
            12.1,
            1.05
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Running Dijkstra from every source costs O(V (V + E) log V).",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Floyd-Warshall runs in Θ(V^3) and tolerates negative edges without negative cycles.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Johnson's algorithm reweights the graph, then runs Dijkstra once per source.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "title": "A* Search",
      "size": [
        13.333,
        7.5
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "A* Search",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.45,
            12.1,
            1.05
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "A* orders the queue by f(v) = g(v) + h(v): cost so far plus an estimate to the goal.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "The heuristic must be admissible, meaning it never overestimates the remaining cost.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "With a consistent heuristic, A* never needs to reopen a closed vertex.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "title": "Practical Notes",
      "size": [
        13.333,
        7.5
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Practical Notes",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.45,
            12.1,
            1.05
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Sparse road networks use Dijkstra with a 4-ary heap plus bidirectional search.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Contraction hierarchies precompute shortcuts and answer continental queries in microseconds.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Distances are stored in a hash map keyed by vertex id, because real graphs are huge.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "title": "Choosing the Algorithm",
      "size": [
        13.333,
        7.5
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Choosing the Algorithm",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.45,
            12.1,
            1.05
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Unweighted graph: BFS.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Non-negative weights: Dijkstra.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Negative weights: Bellman-Ford, or Johnson for all pairs.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "title": "Summary",
      "size": [
        13.333,
        7.5
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Summary",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.45,
            12.1,
            1.05
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "BFS for unweighted graphs, Dijkstra for non-negative weights, Bellman-Ford when edges can be negative.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "The priority queue dominates the running time.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "An admissible heuristic turns a blind search into a guided one.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.85,
            1.85,
            11.6,
            5.0
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    }
